    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you give some specific examples of foods that fall into each category? Like, what are some common high GI items vs low GI items I should look out for?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! High GI foods typically include white bread, sugary cereals, and white rice. These foods have a GI value of 70 or above. Examples of low GI foods are most fruits and vegetables, beans, lentils, and whole grains like quinoa, barley, and oats. Low GI foods have a GI value of 55 or less. Balancing the two in your diet can help manage your blood sugar levels if you are trying to control diabetes. Aiming for low GI choices when it comes to carbohydrates can really make a difference.",
      "source": "target",
