    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. i also wanted to ask, is there a way to combine them so the high GI stuff doesn't spike the sugar as much? or is it better to just avoid them entirely?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Mixing high GI and low GI foods can be a useful strategy to moderate blood sugar spikes. Incorporating the low GI element into a meal with high GI foods can help mitigate the overall impact. For example, you might eat a salsa-based meal (low GI) with white rice (high GI), as the salsa can help slow the digestion process of the rice and keep blood sugar levels more stable. However, chronic overconsumption of high GI foods can still hinder diabetes control, so it's important to prioritize low GI foods in your diet and make them the main component of your carbohydrate intake. The idea is to minimize the high GI food portion rather than completely eliminating it. Balancing your overall diet, including the amount and timing of high GI foods, can help with better blood sugar management. It’s admirable to strive for balance in moderation, but always consult with a dietitian or healthcare provider for personalized dietary advice, especially when managing a health condition like type 2 diabetes.",
      "source": "target",
